{"step_index":8,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T13:17:07Z","content":"**Medical Allied Health Sciences High Yield Revision** **Comprehensive 45 Question Master Bank 02 Mark Definitions Features Classifications** **45 EXAM QUESTIONS 2 MARK STANDARD FORMAT RESPIRATORY CVS ICU DERMATOLOGY OB GYN** --- **PART I RESPIRATORY PATHOLOGY PULMONARY DISORDERS** --- **01 Bronchial Asthma** **Definition** A chronic inflammatory disorder of the airways characterized by bronchial hyperresponsiveness chronic inflammation and recurrent episodes of reversible airflow obstruction and bronchospasm **Common Triggers** Inhaled allergens pollen dust mites animal dander viral respiratory tract infections cold ambient air physical exertion atmospheric smoke pollutants and medications aspirin non selective beta blockers --- **02 Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease COPD** **Definition** A common preventable and treatable pulmonary disease characterized by persistent usually progressive airflow limitation caused by a combination of small airway disease obstructive bronchiolitis and parenchymal destruction emphysema **Major Risk Factors** Tobacco cigarette smoking primary risk factor exposure to biomass fuel smoke and poorly ventilated indoor cooking occupational dusts and chemical fumes ambient air pollution and genetic alpha 1 antitrypsin deficiency --- **03 Tuberculosis TB** **Definition** A chronic communicable granulomatous bacterial infection caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis primarily affecting the pulmonary parenchyma but capable of hematogenous or lymphatic dissemination to extrapulmonary organs **Common Modes of Transmission** Inhalation of microscopic airborne droplet nuclei 1 to 5 um in diameter expelled when a patient with active pulmonary or laryngeal tuberculosis coughs sneezes speaks or sings --- **04 Bronchiectasis** **Definition** A chronic pulmonary disorder characterized by permanent abnormal dilation and destruction of the elastic and muscular components of the bronchial walls driven by a vicious cycle of recurrent infection and transmural inflammation **Common Clinical Features** Chronic persistent cough productive of copious foul smelling purulent sputum recurrent hemoptysis progressive dyspnea coarse inspiratory crackles and rhonchi on auscultation and digital clubbing --- **05 Emphysema** **Definition** An anatomically defined pulmonary disorder characterized by abnormal permanent enlargement of the airspaces distal to the terminal bronchioles accompanied by destruction of alveolar walls without gross fibrosis **Major Pathological Changes** Proteolytic breakdown of alveolar elastin fibers loss of alveolar gas exchanging surface area loss of radial elastic traction causing expiratory airway collapse pulmonary capillary bed depletion and bullae formation --- **06 Pleural Effusion** **Definition** An abnormal accumulation of fluid within the pleural space exceeding the physiological volume 15 mL reflecting an imbalance between pleural fluid production and lymphatic absorption **Common Causes** **Transudative** Congestive heart failure liver cirrhosis with ascites nephrotic syndrome severe hypoalbuminemia **Exudative** Parapneumonic bacterial effusion pulmonary tuberculosis malignancy bronchogenic carcinoma metastases pulmonary embolism --- **07 Pneumothorax** **Definition** The abnormal presence or accumulation of free air or gas within the pleural cavity between the parietal and visceral pleura disrupting negative intrapleural pressure and causing partial or complete ipsilateral lung collapse **Major Types** **Spontaneous** Primary rupture of subpleural apical blebs in healthy young adults or Secondary complication of COPD TB abscess or cystic fibrosis **Traumatic** Penetrating or blunt thoracic trauma including iatrogenic causes central venous catheterization pleural tap **Tension** Medical emergency where a one way valve effect builds positive intrapleural pressure shifts the mediastinum and compromises venous return --- **08 Respiratory Failure** **Definition** An acute or chronic failure of the respiratory system to maintain adequate gas exchange defined objectively as arterial hypoxemia PaO2 60 mmHg with or without hypercapnia PaCO2 50 mmHg at rest on room air at sea level **Major Types** **Type 1 Hypoxemic** PaO2 60 mmHg with normal or reduced PaCO2 caused by V / Q mismatch or shunt eg ARDS severe pneumonia acute pulmonary edema **Type 2 Hypercapnic** PaCO2 50 mmHg with concurrent hypoxemia caused by alveolar hypoventilation or ventilatory pump failure eg severe COPD Guillain Barre opioid overdose --- **09 Interstitial Lung Disease ILD** **Definition** A heterogeneous group of non neoplastic pulmonary disorders characterized by diffuse chronic inflammation cellular infiltration and progressive collagen deposition / fibrosis within the alveolar walls and perilobular interstitium **Common Types** Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis IPF connective tissue disease associated ILD systemic sclerosis rheumatoid arthritis sarcoidosis hypersensitivity pneumonitis and occupational pneumoconioses silicosis asbestosis --- **10 Cystic Fibrosis CF** **Definition** An autosomal recessive multisystem disorder caused by mutations in the CFTR gene on chromosome 7 leading to abnormal epithelial chloride and bicarbonate transport and secretion of viscous mucus **Major Respiratory Manifestations** Thick endobronchial mucus obstruction recurrent chronic bacterial infections Pseudomonas aeruginosa S aureus diffuse bronchiectasis recurrent hemoptysis and chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyposis --- **PART II CARDIOVASCULAR PATHOLOGY HEMODYNAMICS** --- **11 Atherosclerosis** **Definition** A chronic progressive inflammatory disease of large and medium sized elastic and muscular arteries characterized by the formation of fibrofatty intimal plaques atheromas that compromise lumen caliber and vessel elasticity **Major Risk Factors** **Modifiable** Dyslipidemia elevated LDL low HDL systemic hypertension cigarette smoking diabetes mellitus visceral obesity and physical inactivity **Non modifiable** Advancing age male sex and familial or genetic predisposition to premature coronary artery disease --- **12 Thrombosis and Embolism** **Thrombosis** The pathological formation of a solid or semisolid mass thrombus composed of fibrin aggregated platelets and cellular elements within the unruptured vascular tree or cardiac chambers during life **Embolism** The physical occlusion of a vascular lumen by a detached intravascular physical mass thromboembolus fat droplet air bubble amniotic fluid carried by the blood current away from its point of origin --- **13 Ischemic Heart Disease IHD** **Definition** A spectrum of cardiac disorders resulting from an absolute or relative imbalance between myocardial oxygen demand and coronary arterial blood supply predominantly caused by fixed atherosclerotic narrowing **Major Risk Factors** Hypercholesterolemia dyslipidemia systemic arterial hypertension tobacco cigarette smoking diabetes mellitus metabolic syndrome advancing age and family history of early ischemic heart disease --- **14 Congenital Heart Disease CHD** **Definition** Macroscopic structural or functional defects of the heart or great intrathoracic vessels that are present at birth resulting from faulty embryonic cardiovascular morphogenesis **Classification** **Acyanotic Left to Right Shunt / Obstructive** Ventricular Septal Defect VSD Atrial Septal Defect ASD Patent Ductus Arteriosus PDA Coarctation of the Aorta Pulmonary Stenosis **Cyanotic Right to Left Shunt** Tetralogy of Fallot TOF Transposition of the Great Arteries TGA Tricuspid Atresia Truncus Arteriosus Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Return TAPVR --- **15 Rheumatic Heart Disease RHD** **Definition** Chronic permanent cardiac valvular damage resulting from single or recurrent episodes of acute rheumatic fever an autoimmune inflammatory reaction triggered by Group A beta hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis **Common Complications** Chronic valvular stenoses or regurgitations predominantly mitral and aortic valves congestive cardiac failure atrial fibrillation with atrial thrombus formation infective endocarditis and cardioembolic stroke --- **16 Cardiac Failure Heart Failure** **Definition** A complex clinical syndrome caused by structural or functional impairment of ventricular filling or blood ejection rendering the heart incapable of meeting the metabolic demands of tissues at normal venous pressures **Major Types** **By Ejection Fraction** Heart Failure with Reduced EF HFrEF LVEF 40 vs Heart Failure with Preserved EF HFpEF LVEF 50 **By Ventricular Dominance** Left sided heart failure pulmonary congestion vs Right sided heart failure systemic venous congestion **By Time Course** Acute decompensated heart failure vs Chronic stable heart failure --- **17 Cardiac Arrest** **Definition** The abrupt cessation of effective mechanical cardiac pump activity resulting in the immediate collapse of systemic blood flow characterized by unresponsiveness absent carotid pulse and apnea or agonal breathing **Immediate Emergency Response** Confirm arrest shout for help and activate the emergency response system Code Blue / AED retrieval Initiate immediate high quality CPR chest compressions at 100 120 / min 5 6 cm depth 30 2 ratio with ventilations Attach AED / defibrillator analyze rhythm and deliver immediate DC shock if shockable VF or pulseless VT --- **18 Cardiomyopathy** **Definition** A diverse group of intrinsic myocardial disorders associated with mechanical and / or electrical cardiac dysfunction not secondary to coronary disease systemic hypertension valvular lesions or congenital heart disease **Major Types** Dilated Cardiomyopathy DCM Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy HCM Restrictive Cardiomyopathy RCM and Arrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Cardiomyopathy ARVC --- **19 Infective Endocarditis IE** **Definition** A serious invasive microbial infection of the endocardial surface of the heart characterized by the formation of bulky friable vegetations composed of fibrin platelets and microorganisms on the cardiac valves **Common Complications** Acute valvular regurgitation congestive heart failure perivalvular ring abscesses systemic septic embolization cerebral stroke splenic / renal infarction mycotic aneurysms and glomerulonephritis --- **20 Shock** **Definition** A life threatening state of acute systemic circulatory failure characterized by inadequate tissue and cellular microvascular perfusion resulting in cellular dysoxia anaerobic metabolism and progressive multi organ failure **Major Types** **Hypovolemic** Depletion of intravascular volume acute hemorrhage severe dehydration plasma loss in burns **Cardiogenic** Primary pump failure acute myocardial infarction severe acute myocarditis malignant arrhythmias **Distributive** Profound systemic vasodilation septic shock anaphylactic shock neurogenic spinal shock **Obstructive** Physical mechanical impedance to cardiac filling or output cardiac tamponade tension pneumothorax pulmonary embolism --- **PART III EMERGENCY CARE CRITICAL CARE CLINICAL DIAGNOSTICS** --- **21 Hemorrhage** **Definition** The escape or extravasation of whole blood from the intravascular lumen into extravascular tissues body cavities or the external environment following disruption trauma or ulceration of vessel walls **Major Types** **By Vessel** Arterial pulsatile bright red Venous steady dark red Capillary generalized oozing **By Location** External visible bleeding vs Internal hidden in serous cavities / tissues hemothorax hemoperitoneum **By Timing** Primary at time of trauma Reactionary within 24 hours Secondary 7 14 days later due to sepsis --- **22 Septicemia** **Definition** A severe life threatening systemic infection caused by the active multiplication and persistence of virulent bacteria and their toxins in the circulating blood precipitating a dysregulated systemic host inflammatory response **Common Clinical Features** High swinging fever with shaking chills rigors or hypothermia 36 C resting tachycardia 90 bpm tachypnea 20 bpm systemic arterial hypotension altered mental status diaphoresis and progressive oliguria --- **23 Basic Life Support BLS** **Definition** The standardized level of non invasive emergency medical care provided to victims of cardiopulmonary arrest acute airway obstruction or drowning prior to the availability of advanced cardiac life support ACLS **Main Components** Early recognition of cardiac arrest and activation of emergency response high quality cardiopulmonary resuscitation CPR with emphasis on chest compressions early automated external defibrillation AED and foreign body airway clearance maneuvers --- **24 Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation CPR** **Definition** An emergency life saving procedure consisting of external rhythmic manual chest compressions combined with positive pressure rescue ventilations delivered to a victim in cardiac or respiratory arrest **Primary Purpose** To maintain artificial forward perfusion to the coronary arteries and brain sustaining minimal vital organ cellular oxygenation and viability until spontaneous circulation ROSC is restored or definitive therapy is delivered --- **25 Mechanical Ventilation** **Definition** An advanced supportive medical intervention in which an automated machine ventilator is used to assist or completely replace spontaneous pulmonary gas exchange and work of breathing **Two Major Indications** Acute respiratory failure refractory to conservative therapy severe hypoxemic ARDS or acute hypercapnic COPD decompensation Airway protection and loss of neurological respiratory drive severe coma with GCS 8 acute drug toxicity or general anesthesia --- **26 Arterial Blood Gas ABG Analysis** **Definition** A quantitative point of care laboratory evaluation performed on whole arterial blood commonly sampled from the radial artery to measure partial pressures of gases PaO2 PaCO2 pH and bicarbonate concentrations HCO3 **Clinical Significance** Provides rapid objective assessment of alveolar ventilation pulmonary oxygenation efficiency and acid base homeostasis metabolic / respiratory acidosis and alkalosis guiding ventilator management and critical care therapy --- **27 Electrocardiogram ECG** **Definition** A non invasive diagnostic recording of the bioelectrical potentials and cardiac vector voltages generated by myocardial depolarization and repolarization recorded through skin surface electrodes **Major Waves** **P wave** Atrial depolarization **QRS complex** Rapid ventricular depolarization and masked atrial repolarization **T wave** Ventricular repolarization U wave Inconstant repolarization of Purkinje fibers / papillary muscles --- **28 Oxygen Therapy** **Definition** The controlled medical administration of supplemental oxygen at concentrations higher than ambient air FiO2 21 to prevent treat or reverse tissue hypoxia and alleviate cardiopulmonary work **Common Indications** Documented acute hypoxemia SpO2 90 or PaO2 60 mmHg acute myocardial infarction systemic shock states severe trauma and acute carbon monoxide poisoning --- **PART IV DERMATOLOGY INFECTIOUS DISEASES** --- **29 Psoriasis** **Definition** A chronic immune mediated non contagious systemic inflammatory dermatosis driven by T helper lymphocytes resulting in marked hyperproliferation and abnormal differentiation of epidermal keratinocytes **Characteristic Clinical Feature** Sharply demarcated erythematous plaques topped with silvery white micaceous scales on extensor surfaces exhibiting pinpoint capillary bleeding upon scale removal Auspitz sign and lesion development at sites of injury Koebner phenomenon --- **30 Eczema Dermatitis** **Definition** A non contagious inflammatory skin disorder characterized histologically by epidermal spongiosis and clinically by intense pruritus erythema edema and vesiculation acutely progressing to lichenification chronically **Common Types** Atopic dermatitis Allergic contact dermatitis Irritant contact dermatitis Seborrheic dermatitis and Dyshidrotic eczema pompholyx --- **31 Urticaria Hives** **Definition** A transient vascular cutaneous reaction characterized by the abrupt appearance of intensely pruritic erythematous edematous plaques with central pallor wheals caused by mast cell histamine release **Common Triggers** Food allergens shellfish nuts eggs drugs penicillin NSAIDs ACE inhibitors viral or parasitic infections insect envenomation and physical triggers dermographism heat cold localized pressure --- **32 Acne Acne Vulgaris** **Definition** A chronic inflammatory disease of the pilosebaceous units driven by follicular hyperkeratinization sebum overproduction under androgenic influence Cutibacterium acnes proliferation and perifollicular inflammation **Common Types** **Non inflammatory** Open comedones blackheads and closed comedones whiteheads **Inflammatory** Papular acne pustular acne and nodulocystic acne including severe forms like acne conglobata --- **33 Vitiligo** **Definition** An acquired progressive chronic autoimmune skin disease characterized by the targeted immune mediated destruction of epidermal melanocytes leading to complete absence of melanin pigment **Characteristic Clinical Presentation** Asymptomatic well circumscribed chalk white or amelanotic macules and patches of normal cutaneous texture frequently exhibiting bilateral symmetrical distribution over extensor surfaces hands and periorificial areas --- **34 Leprosy Hansens Disease** **Definition** A chronic granulomatous infection caused by Mycobacterium leprae an obligate intracellular acid fast bacillus displaying unique tropism for the skin upper respiratory mucosa and peripheral nerve Schwann cells **Major Neurological Complications** Peripheral sensorimotor neuropathy with painless glove and stocking sensory loss nerve thickening neuropathic plantar ulcerations and peripheral paralyses claw hand ulnar nerve wrist drop radial nerve foot drop common peroneal nerve and lagophthalmos facial nerve --- **PART V CLINICAL NUTRITION MATERNAL HEALTH OB GYN** --- **35 Protein Energy Malnutrition PEM** **Definition** A broad spectrum of nutritional deficiency disorders resulting from insufficient dietary intake poor absorption or hypercatabolic wasting of proteins and energy needed to sustain growth and physiological homeostasis **Major Types** **Kwashiorkor** Predominant protein deficiency with adequate carbohydrates presents with generalized pitting edema flaky paint dermatosis sparse depigmented hair moon facies and hepatomegaly **Marasmus** Severe deficiency of both total calories and protein presents with extreme somatic muscle wasting loss of subcutaneous fat and wizened old man facies without edema **Marasmic Kwashiorkor** Severe muscle and fat wasting combined with peripheral edema --- **36 Vitamin Deficiency Disease** **Definition** A specific pathologic nutritional disorder caused by chronic inadequate dietary intake impaired gastrointestinal absorption or metabolic derangement of one or more vital micronutrients vitamins **Two Examples** **Scurvy** Vitamin C ascorbic acid deficiency impaired collagen synthesis resulting in swollen bleeding gums perifollicular petec